  "answer": "Let me verify each requirement against the implementation:\n\n**`DEFAULT_OPTIONS`** — exposes `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' }`. ✓ Never mutated: `resolveOptions` builds a fresh `resolved` object and never writes to `DEFAULT_OPTIONS`. ✓\n\n**`resolveOptions()` / `resolveOptions(overrides)`** — returns a fresh object each call (new `resolved` literal), defaults overridden by supplied own fields (uses `hasOwnProperty` checks). ✓\n\n**Rejections:**\n- non-object / null / array → `TypeError` ✓ (the `typeof !== 'object'`, `=== null`, `Array.isArray` checks)\n- `undefined` means no overrides ✓ (default param + explicit guard)\n- unknown keys → `TypeError` ✓ (loop over `Object.keys`)\n- precision not integer 0–4 → `RangeError` ✓\n- prefix / unitLabel not string → `TypeError` ✓\n\n**`formatValue`** — accepts only finite numbers (`TypeError` otherwise) ✓; delegates to `resolveOptions` ✓; preserves old call forms ✓.\n\n**Behavior checks:**\n- `formatValue(12.345)` → precision 2, prefix '', unitLabel '' → `\"12.35\"` ✓\n- `formatValue(12.34, { precision: 1, prefix: '~' })` → `\"~12.3\"` ✓\n- `formatValue(12.3, { unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `\"12.30 kg\"` ✓\n- `formatValue(12.3, { precision: 1, prefix: '~', unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `\"~12.3 kg\"` ✓\n\n**No shared mutable state** — `resolveOptions` always returns a new object; `formatValue` doesn't return the options object. ✓\n\nOne subtlety: the `precision` check uses `typeof precision !== 'number' || !Number.isInteger(...)`. Since `Number.isInteger` already returns `false` for non-numbers, the `typeof` guard is redundant but harmless. The `RangeError` is correctly thrown for out-of-range or non-integer values.\n\nAll requirements are satisfied. The three modules are updated:\n\n- **`defaults.mjs`**: `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` now includes `unitLabel: ''`.\n- **`resolver.mjs`**: `resolveOptions` validates input, rejects unknown keys, validates each field type/range, and returns a fresh object without mutating `DEFAULT_OPTIONS`.\n- **`formatter.mjs`**: `formatValue` validates finite numbers, delegates to `resolveOptions`, and appends the unit label when nonempty.\n\nThe existing exports and old call forms are preserved, and the new `unitLabel` option works across all three modules.",
